# Attack-path analysis
Final: high | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Raised from the original medium to high in this repository context because the affected code is in the main product, the threat model explicitly treats valid imported configs that silently enable auto-post to attacker Discord as a high-impact class, and the static path is complete: attacker-controlled config is loaded by OpenSettings, sensitive Discord/auto-post fields are hidden by tab gating, global Save persists the full config, and backend watcher restart can exfiltrate future images. It is not critical because exploitation requires user interaction, has no inbound network exposure or RCE, validates destination to Discord webhooks, and primarily affects future supported image files.
## Likelihood
medium - The attack requires social engineering and user save interaction, but config.json import/drop is a supported workflow and the security-sensitive fields are hidden by default behind inactive tabs, making the review failure plausible.
## Impact
high - Successful exploitation can disclose future local VRChat photos or screenshots to an attacker-controlled Discord channel. These images may contain private user activity or sensitive screen contents. Impact is reduced because it does not execute code, does not grant filesystem-wide read of existing files, and initially marks existing watched images as seen.
## Assumptions
- The victim can be persuaded to open or drag-and-drop an attacker-supplied config.json, which is a documented product workflow.
- The victim clicks the global Save button without explicitly visiting the Discord and auto-post tabs.
- The attacker controls a valid Discord webhook URL and the victim host can make outbound HTTPS requests to Discord.
- The victim later creates new supported image files in a watched VRChat photo or screenshot directory while the app is running.
- Attacker supplies a crafted local JSON config file
- User opens/drops the JSON config or launches the app with it
- User saves settings while sensitive tabs are not reviewed
- Saved config enables autoPhoto or screenshotAutoPost with a valid Discord webhook
- New supported image files appear in the configured watched directory
## Path
attacker config.json -> user opens/drops config -> settings starts on Output tab -> Discord/auto-post settings hidden -> user clicks global Save -> SaveConfig persists all fields -> watchers restart -> new images uploaded to attacker Discord webhook
## Path evidence
- `src/main.go:205-210` - Wails desktop app enables file drop and binds the Go App object to the frontend bridge.
- `src/main.go:71-83` - Launching with a single .json argument loads that file as a config and opens settings mode.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:341-345` - Dropping exactly one .json path calls api.OpenSettings on that attacker-supplied path.
- `src/app.go:262-281` - OpenSettings loads the specified config path, switches the app config path, and places the config into UI state.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:174-176` - Every settings session records a baseline and resets the active tab to output.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:945-955` - The primary Discord webhook field is rendered only when settingsTab is discord.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:959-1013` - Auto-post enable flags, watched directories, and per-feature webhook URLs are rendered only when settingsTab is autoPost.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:623-635` - The Save action submits the full state.config object to SaveConfig or SaveConfigAndProcess, not only the visible tab.
- `src/frontend/src/main.js:1015-1017` - The footer Save button is outside the tab-gated sections and remains available while sensitive fields are hidden.
- `src/app.go:239-247` - Backend save persists the supplied config into state/storage and immediately restarts auto-photo watchers.
- `src/app.go:457-475` - Enabled VRChat photo or screenshot auto-post settings start background watchers using saved directories and webhook URLs.
- `src/internal/appcore/autophoto.go:121-126` - Watcher processing forces Discord upload and applies the configured watcher webhook URL before processing the new file.
- `src/internal/appcore/discord.go:176-187` - A control exists: webhook URLs must be HTTPS Discord webhook URLs, limiting exfiltration destination to Discord webhooks but not preventing attacker-controlled Discord channels.
- `src/internal/appcore/autophoto.go:39-45` - A mitigating behavior exists: watcher startup initially records existing files as seen, so the primary impact is future new images rather than immediate upload of all existing files.
## Narrative
This is a real, reachable security issue in the main desktop app. The app supports opening config.json files, and OpenSettings loads the specified local config into application state. The commit changes the settings page so each session resets to the Output tab and Discord/auto-post controls are rendered only when their tabs are selected. However, the footer Save button remains global and saveSettings submits the entire state.config object. The backend then persists that config and restarts enabled auto-post watchers. Those watchers force Discord upload and use the configured per-feature webhook override. An attacker who persuades a user to open and save a crafted config can therefore hide attacker-controlled webhook and background-upload settings from the initial review path and receive future VRChat photos or screenshots. There is no inbound network service or code execution, and existing images are initially marked seen by the watcher, but the data-exfiltration impact is still meaningful.
## Controls
- No public ingress or listening service identified; this is a local desktop/Wails workflow.
- Victim interaction is required: open/drop the config and click Save.
- Discord webhook URL validation restricts uploads to https://discord.com or discordapp.com /api/webhooks paths.
- Auto-post scans are bounded by MaxAutoPhotoScanFiles and MaxAutoPhotoProcessPerTick.
- Watcher startup marks existing files as seen, limiting impact mostly to future new images.
- Config writes use private file mode where the operating system honors it.
- No executable sink was identified in this path; the sink is image processing plus Discord upload.
## Blindspots
- Static-only analysis; the full Wails GUI was not run interactively in this stage.
- Actual Windows WebView rendering, focus behavior, and user-visible warnings were not observed dynamically.
- The validation PoC was considered from prior evidence but not re-executed during this static pass.
- Windows ACL effectiveness for private config/history storage was not verified.
- Real-world severity depends on how often users import third-party configs and keep the app running while creating sensitive screenshots/photos.